// Twin of input/regression/part-combine-tuplet-end.ly.
// texidoc: "End tuplets events are sent to the starting context, so even after a switch,
// a tuplet ends correctly."
//
// The switch this book is about: bar 1 is part one alone (part two writes R1) = Solo,
// bar 2 has both parts on the same g1 = a2. The SECOND tuplet ends exactly at that
// boundary, so if the end event went to the context the combiner switched TO, the
// tuplet would never close.
// What LP engraves (measured, audit/lpreg/pctend.log): TWO tuplets, each a beamed
// triplet with an EMPTY bracket extent (bracket-visibility = if-no-beam) and a number
// "3" over the middle stem, and nothing of either tuplet reaching bar 2.
//
// LP `g'` under a bare \relative (reference f = F3) is G4 = Lily# `g`.
// The manual beams are kept: a [ ] is a beam EVENT in LilyPond and so enters the
// combiner's span state (same reason as pcglobal-probe.lys).
octave absolute
time 4/4

part vone { clef treble }
part vtwo { clef treble }

section A {
  vone { r2 tuplet 3/2 { g8[ g g] } tuplet 3/2 { g[ g g] } | g1 | }
  vtwo { R1 | g1 | }
}

form main { ~A }

score main { combinedStaff { vone vtwo } }
